<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Sesame agent entitlements.

	  Hardened Runtime is applied via `codesign -o runtime` (a signing flag, not
	  an entitlement) — required for notarization and to run SE crypto under a
	  trusted runtime.

	  NOT sandboxed: the agent writes the ciphertext vault under Application
	  Support and opens a Unix-domain socket in the login session — both of which
	  the App Sandbox would block. So the sandbox entitlement is explicitly false.

	  keychain-access-groups IS required for the biometric/Secure-Enclave gate.
	  Earlier belief (that a signed app reaches its OWN SE key with no group) is
	  DISPROVEN by a reproduced -34018 errSecMissingEntitlement on BOTH the
	  Secure-Enclave key create (SecKeyCreateRandomKey) and a biometric
	  SecAccessControl on a plain keychain item. macOS only honors the
	  keychain/biometry entitlement when the app is signed by an identity that
	  authorizes a TEAM-PREFIXED keychain access group. So the SE/Touch-ID gate
	  REQUIRES this group AND a Developer-ID (or provisioned) signature — an
	  ad-hoc / bare Apple-Development build fails -34018 on add and error 163 on
	  launch when this group is present.

	  $(TEAM_ID) is a PLACEHOLDER — it is NOT hardcoded. scripts/build-app.sh
	  derives the team id from the Developer ID signing identity at build time and
	  substitutes it into a temp copy of this file before `codesign`. The literal
	  `$(TEAM_ID)` string must never be signed as-is.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<false/>
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(TEAM_ID).dev.sesame.app</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
